    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cross Fork grew from a 1893 sawmill by the Lackawanna Lumber Company, attracting 800 workers and 30 businesses by September 1894.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cross_fork"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town once boasted 800 laborers and 120 homes, making it the largest logging town in Pennsylvania before timber ran out.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cross_fork"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1913 Cross Fork had become a ghost town after the lumber company left and the mill burned.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://lumberheritage.org/heritage/wood-on-glass/railroad-through-cross-fork-potter-county-2/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Cross Fork sits at 1,066 feet on the Allegheny Plateau, where Cross Fork Creek meets Kettle Creek within Susquehannock State Forest.",
